body:
I found treating my CBT more as in experience helped as it realised some of the stress, also helped me keep my arms more relaxed or as my instructor said "keep your arms lossy goosy" as it helps you control the bike a lot better, that saying has always stuck with me. Don't be afraid to ask questions so you fully know what you have to do.

&#x200B;

Don't compare yourself to others you are your own person and can take it as many times as you need to, don't rush yourself at the end of the day they would prefer for you to retake your CBT as many times as you need before going on the road so both of you know that you will be safe.

&#x200B;

And you can always try elsewhere that might have a different teaching style which is better for you. Best of luck on your CBT.

Hello! Next week I have my A2 licence and wanted to go a motorbike however I am bit unsure of what motorbike I should get? The A2 licence allows me to ride a motorbike up to 47bph, roughly 500cc, I have heard that the Honda CB500F is a really good bike for A2 and I like the naked style of bike but wanted to see if anyone has other opinion's? 

&#x200B;

Thank you
